 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "LM337 is a three-terminal negative linear regulator whose output is set anywhere from -1.2 V to -37 V by two resistors. It delivers more than 1.5 A. Uses given: on-card local regulation, a programmable-output regulator, and, with a single fixed resistor from Adjust to Vout, a precision current regulator. One adjustable part replaces a stock of fixed-voltage parts."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Current limiting, constant over temperature, thermal overload shutdown and safe-area compensation of the output transistor are all on chip. Power dissipation is rated as internally limited for both packages."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Floating reference",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The regulator holds a nominal -1.25 V, Vref, from its output to its Adjust pin. R1 turns that into a fixed current, IPROG, which also flows from ground through R2, and the drop across R2 sets the output. Only the voltage across the regulator matters to it, not the voltage to ground, so it can float at high voltages."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Adjustment current and minimum load",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "IAdj enters the output equation as an error of IAdj × R2, and the regulator keeps it below 100 µA and nearly constant. It does this by returning all of its quiescent current through the Vout pin, so the load has to draw at least a minimum current (1.5 mA typ, 6.0 mA max for |VI-VO| up to 10 V; 2.5 mA typ, 10 mA max up to 40 V). Below that load the output voltage rises."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input capacitor",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "A 1.0 µF tantalum bypass on the input lowers the regulator's sensitivity to the impedance of the input line. It is needed when the regulator is more than 4 inches from the supply's filter capacitor; a 1.0 µF solid tantalum or a 10 µF aluminium electrolytic is the choice."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Adjustment capacitor",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "CAdj from Adjust to ground keeps the ripple from growing with the output setting. In a 10 V application a 10 µF part raises the 120 Hz ripple rejection by about 15 dB (60 dB typ without, 77 dB typ with)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output capacitor",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "CO is required for stability. It is a 1.0 µF tantalum or 10 µF aluminium electrolytic part of the conventional type, with ESR not reduced. Capacitors with reduced ESR, ceramics among them, can leave the regulator unstable or in continuous oscillation."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection diodes",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "External capacitors can discharge back into the regulator through its low-current nodes. For outputs beyond -25 V, or CO above 25 µF, or CAdj above 10 µF, two 1N4002 diodes are added: D1 from Vin to Vout guards against CO discharging when the input is shorted, D2 from Vout to Adjust against CAdj discharging when the output is shorted, and the two together cover CAdj during an input short."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Load regulation layout",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Wiring resistance between R1 and the regulator adds to the reference voltage and spoils regulation, so R1 is placed right at the regulator. Taking the ground end of R2 to the load's ground gives remote sensing of ground and better load regulation."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Test method",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Line and load regulation are specified at a constant junction temperature with pulsed measurement at low duty cycle; the test circuits use about 1% duty cycle."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heatsink",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "On both packages the heatsink surface (terminal 4 of the D2PAK outline, the tab of the TO-220AB) is tied to pin 2, Vin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "D2PAK copper area",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "On a board with 2.0 oz. copper, standing vertically in free air, the D2PAK's junction-to-air resistance falls, and the dissipation it may carry at TA = +50°C rises, as the side L of the square copper pad under its tab grows from 0 to 30 mm. The minimum size pad is L = 10 mm, where RθJA is about 45 °C/W and PD(max) about 2.25 W."
  }
 ],
